<h2>The order under appeal</h2>
<p>A risk-and-cost termination leaves two live wounds: the financial claim, which arbitration will decide, and the reputational entry &mdash; adverse remarks that follow a contractor into every future tender. The contractor moved the Commercial Court under Section 9 and obtained a limited shield: the remarks would stay in abeyance for four months, or until the contractor could ask the arbitral tribunal itself for protection under Section 17, whichever came first. The order did not erase the remarks or adjudicate the termination; it preserved tendering capacity during the gap before a tribunal exists.</p>

<h2>The appeal and its quiet end</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The Union&rsquo;s fear.</strong> With remarks frozen, the contractor could bid for the balance work on the same project it was removed from — an outcome the employer found intolerable.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The concession.</strong> The contractor&rsquo;s counsel stated it would not bid for the balance work.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The disposal.</strong> With the only concrete prejudice addressed, the Division Bench declined to disturb a time-limited interim order and disposed of the appeal in terms of the parties&rsquo; submissions, without touching the merits of the termination or the remarks.</div>
</div>

<h2>What this says about Section 37 practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Practical consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Deference to Section 9 discretion</td><td>Appellate courts rarely re-exercise the interim discretion; a calibrated, time-limited order is hard to dislodge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The Section 17 handover</td><td>Orders framed to last only until the tribunal can act under Section 17 are structurally modest — and therefore resilient on appeal</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Undertakings as currency</td><td>A recorded statement of counsel can resolve an appeal faster and more cheaply than argument; it binds, and breach has consequences</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Merits preserved</td><td>Nothing in the disposal prejudges the arbitration on the termination, the remarks or the risk-and-cost claim</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The Delhi angle</h2>
<p>The same architecture governs commercial arbitration practice before the District Courts and the High Court of Delhi: Section 9 relief bridging the pre-tribunal gap, Section 37(1)(b) appeals within the 60-day discipline of Section 13(1A) of the Commercial Courts Act, and a strong appellate preference for leaving calibrated interim orders alone. Parties aggrieved by an interim measure are often better served by identifying the specific prejudice and negotiating a recorded undertaking than by a full-dress attack on the order.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the Uttarakhand High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice; the arbitration between the parties, if pursued, will decide the merits.</div>
